  Deployment Rubric Assessment

  Privacy-at-Risk: Score 1

  The ZK circuit only ever discloses a Boolean (correct/incorrect). The culprit ID and salt are witness-only — never revealed on-chain. The commitment is a one-way
  hash. The only "sensitive" data is the game outcome, which is a public deduction game result with no real-world identity linkage.

  Rationale: Data is intentionally public or devoid of real-world harm. Game IDs, guess IDs, and win/loss results are the full on-chain footprint.

  ---
  Value-at-Risk: Score 1

  The contract holds zero funds. No token transfers, no escrow, no vault. The only cost to users is transaction fees (DUST), which are sponsored server-side. An
  exploit could corrupt game state (fake commitments, invalid active flags) but cannot drain any user principal.

  Rationale: No funds locked in contract at any time.

  ---
  State-Space-at-Risk: Score 1 (revised from 2)                                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                                      
  The contract now has a delete_game circuit that:                                                                                                                    
  - Requires knowledge of the original culprit_id + salt (ZK-gated — only the sponsor server can call it)                                                             
  - Only permits deletion of inactive games (completed or abandoned)                                                                                                  
  - Calls games.remove() to actually reclaim ledger storage         
                                                                                                                                                                      
  This means the sponsor server can prune games post-completion, giving the map a natural ceiling equal to the number of currently active games rather than all games
  ever played. State is bounded per-session lifecycle.                                                                                                                                                                           

